\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces A nested residue hierarchy. Each residue modulo $2$ is the union of two residues modulo $4$, and each residue modulo $4$ is the union of two residues modulo $8$.}}{6}{figure.caption.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The incidence matrix $M_{r,s}=1$ when the fine residue $s$ belongs to the coarse residue $r$. Its rows implement exact aggregation from fine packets to coarse packets.}}{8}{figure.caption.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces A composite residue packet is addressed by its dyadic and odd coordinates. The displayed mod-$24$ system is the product of mod $8$ and mod $3$.}}{12}{figure.caption.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces A finite packet-transfer graph. Edge weights record filtration gain rather than merely the number of operator compositions.}}{17}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces At precision $L$, weighted inversion requires only transfer words whose cumulative weight is below $L$.}}{19}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The walk counts for one weighted graph. The exact generating function is rational, so these counts satisfy a finite linear recurrence.}}{23}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The rational sign projectors for an integral involution. Their denominator has $2$-adic weight one.}}{33}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The denominator weight of the sign projector is nonzero only at the prime two.}}{34}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces A controlled defect for one step and its telescoping norm bound under repeated operator application.}}{40}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Norms of packet blocks for a finite stencil. The original operator is exactly reconstructed from a sparse transfer pattern.}}{43}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Nested phase orbits at powers of $2$ on the Smith reflection disk. The full treatment of the Smith chart is deferred to the signal chapter.}}{45}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces A symmetric refinement weight concentrated near the boundary points for open and short circuits.}}{46}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces The dependency structure of Chapter II. Coordinate coherence, residue/CRT, weighted inversion, automatic finite-graph transfer rationality, and integral separation are distinct branches with explicit inputs.}}{50}{figure.caption.16}%
